{"name":"Outback Brown","hex":"#7e5d47","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"outback","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The most remote and desolate areas of Australia; the desert and areas too arid for growing crops."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Characteristic of the most remote and desolate areas of Australia; very remote from urban areas."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/outback"},{"term":"brown","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A colour like that of chocolate or coffee.","One of the colour balls used in snooker, with a value of 4 points."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a brown color/colour.","Gloomy."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/brown"}],"entries":[{"title":"Outback","blocks":[{"text":"The Outback is a remote, vast, sparsely populated area of Australia. It is more remote than what Australians call \"the bush\". While often envisaged as being arid, the Outback regions extend from the northern to southern Australian coastlines and encompass a number of climatic zones, including tropical and monsoonal climates in northern areas, arid areas in the \"red centre\" and semi-arid and temperate climates in southerly regions. The total population is estimated at 607,000 people."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Mount_Conner%2C_August_2003.jpg/330px-Mount_Conner%2C_August_2003.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Outback","wikidata":"Q569073"},{"title":"Brown","blocks":[{"text":"Brown is a color. It can be thought as a darker, typically desaturated shade of orange, and can often be produced by combining red and yellow with another color, namely blue."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Espresso-roasted_coffee_beans.jpg/330px-Espresso-roasted_coffee_beans.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","l